IO-34: keep rule-action settings within their valid range

addAction() rejected a numeric orifice, weir or outlet setting outside 0..1
but accepted any numeric pump setting, so "PUMP P1 SETTING = -1" ran the
pump backwards. And the values a CURVE or TIMESERIES action produces were
used as they came: a series that strayed to 3 gave an outlet three times its
rating curve, and a negative value reversed the flow. Only the PID branch
clamped its result. Reject a negative numeric pump setting (ERROR 211, as
for regulators) and clamp every action's value in updateActionValue() to
>= 0, and to <= 1 for a link that is not a pump, as getPIDSetting() does.

Apply from the root of a Stormwater-Management-Model checkout at cb3e192b:
    git apply IO-34_swmm530.patch

diff --git a/src/legacy/engine/controls.c b/src/legacy/engine/controls.c
index e423886..a62b1fa 100644
--- a/src/legacy/engine/controls.c
+++ b/src/legacy/engine/controls.c
@@ -1492,6 +1492,8 @@ int addAction(int r, char *tok[], int nToks)
                                    &attrib, values);
             if (err > 0)
                 return err;
+            if (attrib == r_SETTING && values[0] < 0.0)
+                return error_setInpError(ERR_NUMBER, tok[5]);
         }
         else
             return error_setInpError(ERR_KEYWORD, tok[3]);
@@ -1642,6 +1644,13 @@ void updateActionValue(struct TAction *a, DateTime currentTime, double dt)
     {
         a->value = getPIDSetting(a, dt);
     }
+
+    // --- keep a curve or time series setting within its feasible
+    //     range, as getPIDSetting() does
+    if (a->value < 0.0)
+        a->value = 0.0;
+    if (Link[a->link].type != PUMP && a->value > 1.0)
+        a->value = 1.0;
 }
 
